{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011yJPkC7NdvpfhNMDJ8AP8f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014ZKgW1dgiYcCKtvBdNkRcS","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review: Learning When to Verify: RL-Trained Selective Self-Verification for Interactive Agents\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper makes two claims: (1) it diagnoses why step-independent GRPO training catastrophically fails for multi-turn LLM agents, identifying policy information loss and advantage sign misattribution as dual mechanisms, and proposes episode-level GRPO as a fix; (2) it introduces B-IAVR, a selective verification policy trained with action-change as intrinsic reward, reducing verification cost by 52% with minimal success degradation. The diagnostic contribution is clearly articulated and empirically supported, though the novelty bar is debatable. The selective verification component is less convincing due to the absolute performance gap with existing methods.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-executed mechanistic analysis.** The sign misattribution diagnosis is rigorous: r=0.97 correlation between advantage sign and episode outcome, r=0.04 with local quality. The h-sweep showing a catastrophic cliff at h<=5 is compelling empirical evidence of a non-trivial threshold effect. The formal analysis connecting misattribution severity to baseline success rate (P(correct) approaches p as K grows) is insightful.\n\n- **Honest framing.** The paper explicitly states \"This is not a novel algorithm but a diagnostic insight.\" This intellectual honesty is refreshing and appropriate. The paper does not overclaim.\n\n- **Systematic experimental design.** Multi-seed replication (5 seeds), h-sweep ablation (5 values, 3 seeds each), cross-environment validation (ScienceWorld), model scaling (7B), and causal intervention for action-change signal. The evidence pyramid is well-constructed.\n\n- **Practical relevance of the diagnosis.** The observation that community-default training silently produces 0% success while maintaining high action validity (the agent \"looks fine\" superficially) is genuinely useful for practitioners. The six factors explaining why the community defaulted to the broken paradigm add value.\n\n- **Related work is substantially improved** (in the full paper draft) compared to the 10-reference version described in the task. The expanded related work covers selective verification competitors (Derailer-Rerailer, Sherlock, Agent-Omit, ARES, AutoThink), credit assignment (GAE, PPO, TD-lambda, GiGPO, HCAPO), and self-verification literature (Kamoi survey, Long et al. verification dilemma, CorrectBench, VerifiAgent). This is comprehensive.\n\n- **B-IAVR's causal intervention experiment** (Table 8 in the draft) is a strong methodological choice: verification-triggered changes yield 48% success vs 35% for random changes at matched rate, establishing that action-change captures genuine verification value.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited novelty of the core algorithmic contribution.** Episode-level GRPO is standard REINFORCE applied to full trajectories. The paper acknowledges this but claims the \"diagnostic insight\" is the contribution. However, the insight that windowed training with sparse rewards corrupts credit assignment is arguably well-understood in classical RL. The specific formalization for GRPO is new, but the conceptual point (don't split trajectories into independent windows when reward is episode-level) follows directly from first principles. A reviewer familiar with temporal credit assignment literature would likely find this expected rather than surprising.\n\n- **Absolute performance gap undermines the significance narrative.** The paper achieves 46.8% on ALFWorld with 1.7B. Self-Guide achieves 72.7% (same model), GiGPO achieves 86.1% (1.5B), PA-MoE+GiGPO achieves 93.8% (1.5B), and ReflAct achieves 93.3% (GPT-4 prompting). The paper does not adequately address why its episode-level approach is 26pp below Self-Guide on the same model. The discussion frames this as \"different paradigm (expert-guided vs from-scratch RL)\" but does not explain why this gap matters for the selective verification story. If the underlying agent is weak, the verification routing results are less meaningful.\n\n- **B-IAVR results rest on a weak base agent.** B-IAVR achieves 46.5% success with 48% verification. But the no-verify baseline is 37%. The marginal value of selective verification is therefore small in absolute terms (46.5% vs 37% no-verify vs 48% always-on). On a stronger agent (70%+ base), the cost-accuracy tradeoff would be far more compelling. The success/cost ratio metric (0.75) is somewhat arbitrary and masks the small absolute differences.\n\n- **Incomplete comparison with most relevant competitors.** The paper compares only against ARES (reproduced) but not against Derailer-Rerailer, Agent-Omit, or Thinkless on comparable settings. The related work discusses these methods textually but provides no empirical comparison. For a selective verification paper, head-to-head comparison with Derailer-Rerailer (the closest binary-trigger method) is essential.\n\n- **Sign misattribution analysis is specific to one implementation (RAGEN).** The paper generalizes the finding theoretically but only demonstrates it empirically in RAGEN's codebase. Other multi-turn RL frameworks (e.g., WebAgent-R1's M-GRPO) may handle windowing differently. The claim that \"the community default is broken\" requires showing that multiple frameworks exhibit this behavior, not just one.\n\n- **The two contributions feel disconnected.** The paper is structurally a \"diagnosis paper\" (Sections 1-4) stapled to a \"selective verification paper\" (B-IAVR). The connection is that you need working RL to train B-IAVR, but a reader primarily interested in the diagnosis gets a weakly-supported verification system, and a reader interested in selective verification gets a lengthy diagnostic preamble for a method that underperforms existing approaches in absolute terms.\n\n- **Missing discussion of why episode-level is 26pp below Self-Guide.** Self-Guide uses the same base model and achieves 72.7%. If episode-level GRPO is the \"correct\" training paradigm, why does it plateau at 46.8%? The paper hints at \"expert-guided vs from-scratch\" but does not analyze whether the gap is due to reward sparsity, exploration difficulty, or something else. This gap is the most obvious reviewer attack vector.\n\n- **Selective verification not validated on stronger agents.** The paper acknowledges in limitations that \"selective verification not implemented\" in the full project paper, though the dream/best version includes B-IAVR results. If B-IAVR were trained on top of a Self-Guide-level agent (72.7%), the cost-efficiency story would be far stronger.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Why does episode-level GRPO plateau at 46.8% when Self-Guide achieves 72.7% on the same model? Is this purely a reward sparsity issue, or is episode-level coarse credit the binding constraint at higher performance levels? Have you tried combining episode-level training with Self-Guide's internal reward?\n\n2. The sign misattribution analysis assumes RAGEN's specific implementation of step-independent training. Have you verified that other frameworks (e.g., WebAgent-R1, SPEAR) exhibit the same pathology? If they use different windowing strategies, does your diagnosis still apply?\n\n3. The GAE comparison shows only +3.2pp over episode-level GRPO. But GAE requires a learned value function. What is the computational overhead of GAE in this setting? Is the 3.2pp gain worth the added complexity?\n\n4. For B-IAVR, the action-change reward implicitly assumes that action changes are beneficial. But in a well-trained agent, an action change could also indicate verification-induced \"second-guessing\" that worsens performance. How do you distinguish informative corrections from harmful overthinking?\n\n5. The success/cost ratio metric treats verification cost as a linear fraction of total inference cost. Is this accurate? In practice, verification may involve environment re-observation or additional forward passes with different latency characteristics. What is the actual wall-clock cost reduction?\n\n6. Table 7 shows no-verify achieves 37% while always-on achieves 48%. This 11pp gap means verification genuinely helps. But B-IAVR at 48% verify rate only loses 1.5pp relative to always-on. Does this mean ~50% of verifications are pure waste, or that B-IAVR is correctly identifying and skipping the wasteful ones?\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The paper uses \"pp\" (percentage points) without definition. While common in ML papers, it should be defined at first use.\n- Table 4 caption says \"n=3200 windows from 256 episodes\" but the table rows sum to 3200. Clarify whether this is the full dataset or a sample.\n- The term \"action-change\" is hyphenated inconsistently (sometimes \"action change\", sometimes \"action-change\").\n- The Discussion section in the full project draft includes \"Selective verification not implemented\" in limitations, which contradicts the experiments section. This needs reconciliation.\n- The connection between Mechanism 1 (information loss) and Mechanism 2 (sign misattribution) could be clearer in the Method section. Currently the interaction is only discussed in the Discussion.\n\n## Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\nThe diagnostic contribution is solid and practically useful for the LLM agent training community. The sign misattribution formalization, the h-sweep cliff, and the multi-seed empirical validation together constitute a meaningful contribution. However, the novelty is limited (episode-level RL is not new), the absolute performance significantly trails existing methods, and the selective verification component is insufficiently validated on competitive base agents. The paper is above the acceptance threshold for a diagnostic/analysis contribution but would benefit from either (a) demonstrating that the diagnosis enables a substantially stronger agent when combined with existing techniques, or (b) showing B-IAVR on a competitive base agent where cost-efficiency actually matters.\n\n## Confidence: 4/5\n\nI am confident in the assessment. I have reviewed the related work landscape thoroughly, including recent 2025-2026 papers on agent RL training (GiGPO, PA-MoE, HCAPO, T2PO, SDAR, Agent-Omit) and selective verification (Derailer-Rerailer, Sherlock, ARES, Thinkless, AutoThink). The main uncertainty is whether the community would view the diagnosis as sufficiently novel given classical RL knowledge of credit assignment, which is ultimately a judgment call that could go either way depending on the reviewer pool.\n","project":"cost-verify-agent-rl","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260517T121256","overall_score":"weak accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":53650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MwFPvG1WNufR3GXVFagigh","session_id":"db5a7c35-a1db-4dc3-87e7-9a67292d9dd1","uuid":"e2e567c2-ff64-41eb-9596-39a964c2ea00"}
